cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "The GEO Cookbook"
message: "If you use this cookbook, please cite it."
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: "Aporta Franco"
    given-names: "Fernando"
    alias: ferinazumaDEV
abstract: >-
  Six reproducible experiments on the machine legibility of web content for
  generative answer engines. Each pairs a before/after artifact with a
  deterministic offline script, and reports the measured property with its
  proxy, sample size, confidence and limitations. The measurements are
  machine-legibility proxies only: none of them measures retrieval, reranking,
  generation or citation by any engine.
repository-code: "https://github.com/ferinazumaDEV/generative-engine-optimization-cookbook"
url: "https://zentimes.es"
# ONE license id here, deliberately. This repository IS dual-licensed — prose
# CC BY 4.0 (root LICENSE), code samples MIT (LICENSES/MIT.txt) — and README.md
# is where that is stated. Do not restore the list. This file is what Zenodo
# reads to archive a release, and its deposit format takes a single license id:
# a YAML list here converts to {"id": ["MIT", "CC-BY-4.0"]}, Zenodo rejects the
# deposit, and the release is archived as Failed with no DOI minted. That is
# not a guess — it is what happened to v0.1.0 on 4 Sep 2026, reproduced with
# cffconvert 2.0.0. Keep this value equal to the root LICENSE, which is also
# what GitHub's own license detection reports.
license: CC-BY-4.0
# version and date-released must be bumped together with the git tag; the tag
# is the source of truth, these two are the copy CFF requires.
version: "0.1.1"
date-released: "2026-09-04"
keywords:
  - generative engine optimization
  - GEO
  - AI search
  - answer engines
  - machine legibility
  - reproducible measurement
